# Line endings, pinned so a clone behaves identically on every platform.
#
# This file exists because of a specific failure. Windows Git defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites line endings on checkout. A shell script
# written and committed on Windows therefore arrives on Linux with CRLF, and the
# kernel refuses it:
#
#     bash: ./scripts/build.sh: /bin/bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# That is the classic way a build works for the person who wrote it and for
# nobody else, and it is why "it runs on Linux too" cannot be left to each
# developer's local Git settings.

# Everything is normalised to LF in the repository. Checkout is LF as well, so
# the working tree matches what CI and a Linux clone see.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the one exception: cmd.exe is happiest with CRLF, and
# they are never executed anywhere else.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Never touched. `text=auto` would otherwise be free to mangle these, and a
# corrupted binary fails in a way that looks like a code bug.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.pdb binary
